You are working on the ExamFlow project.

We are now starting:

PHASE 9 — PRODUCTION HARDENING & REGISTRAR WORKFLOW

The goal of this phase is to make ExamFlow suitable for controlled
institutional use by the Registrar's Office and authorized IITS staff.

Previous phases implemented:

Phase 1 — Authentication
Phase 2 — Exam Management
Phase 3 — Student/Roster Management
Phase 4 — Room/Seat Management
Phase 5 — Seating Assignment
Phase 6 — Crowdmark Template Import
Phase 7 — Personalized Exam Generation
Phase 8 — QR Codes, Signature Lists, Seating Maps,
           and Exam Administration Package

This phase is NOT about adding major new business features.

The goal is:

reliability
security
operability
auditability
maintainability
data integrity
administrator usability
production deployment readiness

IMPORTANT:

Do NOT introduce AI.

Do NOT introduce machine learning.

Do NOT introduce RAG.

Do NOT add unnecessary microservices.

Do NOT rewrite the architecture.

Do NOT replace PostgreSQL.

Do NOT replace the existing authentication system.

Do NOT rebuild Crowdmark.

Do NOT implement grading.

Do NOT implement OCR.

Do NOT create a student portal.

Do NOT create a mobile application.

Do NOT add speculative features.

Keep the system simple and professional.

==================================================
1. READ DOCUMENTATION
==================================================

Read:

- doc/prd.md
- doc/Architecture.md
- doc/DATABASE.md
- doc/SECURITY.md
- doc/DEVELOPMENT.md
- doc/AGENTS.md

Review all previous implementation phases.

Before coding:

1. inspect current architecture
2. inspect database schema
3. inspect authentication
4. inspect authorization
5. inspect file storage
6. inspect PDF generation
7. inspect audit logging
8. inspect tests
9. inspect deployment configuration

Provide a concise implementation plan before making changes.

==================================================
2. PRODUCTION PRINCIPLE
==================================================

ExamFlow handles confidential examination information.

Therefore:

Correctness > convenience.

Security > speed.

Predictability > complexity.

Auditability > cleverness.

Do not optimize for features that are not required.

==================================================
3. ENVIRONMENT SEPARATION
==================================================

Clearly support:

development
test
production

Do not allow development settings to accidentally be used in production.

Examples:

DEBUG
development database
development storage
test authentication

must not be enabled accidentally.

==================================================
4. ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
==================================================

Review all environment variables.

Classify:

PUBLIC
SERVER-ONLY
SECRET

Never expose:

database credentials
storage credentials
authentication secrets
API keys
QR signing secrets

to the browser.

==================================================
5. ENVIRONMENT VALIDATION
==================================================

At application startup:

validate required environment variables.

If required production configuration is missing:

fail clearly.

Do not start in an unsafe partial configuration.

==================================================
6. SECRET MANAGEMENT
==================================================

Never commit secrets.

Check:

.gitignore
.env.example
deployment configuration

Ensure:

.env

is excluded.

Provide:

.env.example

containing variable names only.

==================================================
7. DATABASE MIGRATIONS
==================================================

Review all database migrations.

Verify:

alembic upgrade head

works on a clean database.

Verify migrations are ordered correctly.

Do not modify already-applied migrations destructively.

Create new migrations when schema changes are required.

==================================================
8. DATABASE CONSTRAINTS
==================================================

Review critical integrity rules.

Ensure database constraints prevent:

duplicate student assignments
duplicate seats
invalid exam relationships
invalid room relationships
duplicate QR tokens
invalid generated document relationships

Use database constraints rather than relying exclusively on application logic.

==================================================
9. DATABASE INDEXES
==================================================

Review indexes for:

exam_id
student_id
student_id + exam_id
room_id
seat_id
assignment_id
template_id
status
qr_token

Only create indexes that are actually useful.

Do not create indexes everywhere.

==================================================
10. TRANSACTIONS
==================================================

Review all multi-step operations.

Especially:

seating assignment
personalized exam generation
package generation
exam state changes

Ensure partial database updates do not leave the system inconsistent.

==================================================
11. FILE STORAGE
==================================================

Review:

template storage
generated exam storage
package storage

Ensure confidential documents are not stored in:

public/
static/
frontend assets

unless explicitly designed as public.

==================================================
12. FILE ACCESS
==================================================

All protected documents must be accessed through authorized server endpoints.

Do not expose direct storage paths.

Do not expose filesystem paths.

==================================================
13. PATH TRAVERSAL
==================================================

Review every file operation.

Reject unsafe paths.

Never allow user input to become a raw filesystem path.

Use server-generated storage keys.

==================================================
14. SAFE FILENAMES
==================================================

Review all generated filenames.

Ensure:

no ../
no absolute paths
no control characters
no executable extensions

Use deterministic server-generated names.

==================================================
15. PDF VALIDATION
==================================================

Review uploaded templates.

Validate:

file type
file size
PDF structure

Do not trust:

filename extension
Content-Type header alone

==================================================
16. UPLOAD LIMITS
==================================================

Configure reasonable limits for:

PDF upload size
CSV upload size
request size

Do not allow unlimited uploads.

==================================================
17. CSV IMPORT

Provide a professional roster import workflow.

The system should support CSV import if doc/prd.md requires it.

Example:

student_id,name

100000001,Alice Student
100000002,Bob Student

==================================================
18. CSV VALIDATION

Before importing:

validate the entire file.

Check:

required columns
duplicate student IDs
missing student IDs
missing names
invalid encoding
invalid rows

Do not partially import a malformed file.

==================================================
19. IMPORT PREVIEW

Before committing CSV import:

show:

Total rows
Valid rows
Invalid rows
Duplicates

Example:

Students found: 185
Valid: 183
Invalid: 2

Errors:

Row 87:
Missing student ID

Row 141:
Duplicate student ID

Require user confirmation before committing.

==================================================
20. IMPORT TRANSACTION

CSV import should be transactional.

Either:

all valid records are committed

or:

nothing is committed

unless the PRD explicitly defines partial import behavior.

Prefer all-or-nothing.

==================================================
21. CSV ERROR REPORT

Provide an error report that can be downloaded.

Example:

row,error

87,Missing student ID
141,Duplicate student ID

Do not expose unnecessary internal database details.

==================================================
22. EXPORT

Provide controlled export where appropriate.

Possible exports:

roster CSV
seating CSV
room assignment CSV

Do not export confidential documents through CSV.

Respect authorization.

==================================================
23. DATA VALIDATION

Review every important form.

Never trust frontend validation alone.

Server-side validation is mandatory.

==================================================
24. AUTHORIZATION

Review every API endpoint.

Create an authorization matrix.

Example:

                 Admin   Registrar   Instructor
Create Exam        ✓         ✓            -
Import Roster      ✓         ✓            -
Assign Seats       ✓         ✓            ✓*
Generate Exams     ✓         ✓            ✓*
Download Package   ✓         ✓            ✓*

*only for authorized exams

Use the actual roles already defined by the project.

Do not invent roles unless necessary.

==================================================
25. OBJECT-LEVEL AUTHORIZATION

Do not stop at role checking.

Verify:

user has permission to access THIS exam.

This is critical.

==================================================
26. IDOR REVIEW

Audit all endpoints for:

exam_id
student_id
assignment_id
generated_exam_id
template_id
room_id
package_id

Ensure users cannot access another exam by changing an ID.

==================================================
27. ADMINISTRATIVE PRIVILEGES

Do not make every authenticated user an administrator.

Use least privilege.

==================================================
28. AUTHENTICATION HARDENING

Review:

password policy if local authentication exists
session expiration
cookie security
CSRF protection where applicable
login rate limiting
logout behavior
session invalidation

Follow existing institutional authentication architecture.

Do not replace it.

==================================================
29. SSO READINESS

If institutional SSO is expected in the future:

keep authentication behind a clear abstraction.

Do not implement SSO unless explicitly required.

Document the integration point.

==================================================
30. SECURITY HEADERS

Review appropriate:

Content-Security-Policy
X-Content-Type-Options
Referrer-Policy
Strict-Transport-Security

Use values compatible with the application.

Do not blindly add headers that break the application.

==================================================
31. HTTPS

Production deployment must use HTTPS.

Do not send:

login credentials
student information
exam documents

over plain HTTP.

==================================================
32. COOKIE SECURITY

For authentication cookies:

Secure
HttpOnly
SameSite

must be configured appropriately.

==================================================
33. CSRF

If cookie-based authentication is used:

implement appropriate CSRF protection.

If token-based authentication is used:

follow the project's existing security architecture.

Do not add redundant mechanisms without understanding the authentication model.

==================================================
34. RATE LIMITING

Review rate limiting for:

login
file upload
document download
QR verification
package generation

Do not rate-limit normal internal workflows so aggressively that they become
unusable.

==================================================
35. QR RATE LIMITING

QR verification endpoints should have reasonable rate limiting.

Do not allow unlimited automated token probing.

==================================================
36. AUDIT LOGGING

Review audit events.

Important events include:

login
logout
exam creation
exam modification
roster import
room creation
seat assignment
template upload
template activation
personalized exam generation
QR generation
package generation
document download
package download

==================================================
37. AUDIT LOG DESIGN

Audit record should include:

timestamp
actor
action
resource type
resource ID
result

Optional:

IP address

Only store information justified by institutional policy.

==================================================
38. AUDIT IMMUTABILITY

Normal application users must not be able to modify audit records.

If deletion is required:

restrict it to authorized administrative processes.

==================================================
39. AUDIT RETENTION

Do not invent an institutional retention period.

Document that retention should follow university policy.

==================================================
40. LOGGING

Application logs should contain:

request ID
timestamp
severity
service
operation
safe error information

Do NOT log:

passwords
tokens
session cookies
QR tokens
student names unnecessarily
student IDs unnecessarily
exam contents

==================================================
41. ERROR HANDLING

Never return internal stack traces to users.

Bad:

Traceback...
SQLAlchemy...
filesystem path...

Good:

"Unable to generate the examination document. Please contact an administrator."

Log the detailed technical error server-side.

==================================================
42. ERROR CODES

Use consistent error categories.

Examples:

VALIDATION_ERROR
AUTHENTICATION_ERROR
AUTHORIZATION_ERROR
NOT_FOUND
CONFLICT
FILE_ERROR
GENERATION_ERROR
INTERNAL_ERROR

Do not create hundreds of error codes.

==================================================
43. REQUEST IDs

Every server request should have a correlation/request ID.

Include it in error responses when appropriate.

Example:

Request ID:
8f3c...

This helps support staff troubleshoot issues.

==================================================
44. UI ERROR HANDLING

The frontend must display:

what went wrong
what the user can do next

Example:

"3 students are missing seats."

Action:

"Go to Seating"

==================================================
45. GLOBAL ERROR BOUNDARY

Ensure frontend unexpected errors do not expose:

stack traces
environment variables
internal API details

==================================================
46. EMPTY STATES

Every major page should have a useful empty state.

Examples:

No exams yet.

No roster imported.

No rooms configured.

No seating assignments.

No personalized exams generated.

==================================================
47. LOADING STATES

Every long-running operation should show progress or clear loading state.

Especially:

CSV import
exam generation
package generation

==================================================
48. CONFIRMATION DIALOGS

Require confirmation for destructive or high-impact operations:

delete exam
replace roster
regenerate official documents
change seating after documents generated

Do not require confirmation for harmless navigation.

==================================================
49. DESTRUCTIVE OPERATIONS

Prefer:

archive

over:

hard delete

for important exam records.

Follow doc/prd.md.

==================================================
50. EXAM LIFECYCLE

Review exam status.

Recommended concept:

DRAFT
READY
PUBLISHED
COMPLETED
ARCHIVED

Use existing status definitions if already implemented.

Do not introduce unnecessary state complexity.

==================================================
51. STATE VALIDATION

Prevent invalid transitions.

Example:

ARCHIVED → DRAFT

should not happen accidentally.

==================================================
52. LOCKING / FINALIZATION

Before final exam package generation:

allow the exam to be marked:

READY FOR ADMINISTRATION

After that:

changes to roster/seating/template should trigger stale state.

Do not allow silent changes.

==================================================
53. EXAM LOCK

If doc/prd.md supports an exam lock:

make it explicit.

Example:

[Lock Exam for Administration]

After locking:

- roster changes restricted
- seating changes restricted
- template changes restricted

Only authorized users can unlock.

Do not implement a complicated workflow.

==================================================
54. STALE DETECTION

Ensure the system can detect:

roster changed
template changed
seating changed
room changed

after documents were generated.

Show:

"Documents are out of date."

==================================================
55. PRINT WORKFLOW

Make the printing workflow clear.

The system should allow an administrator to:

1. validate exam
2. generate package
3. download package
4. verify package
5. print

Do not attempt to directly control physical printers.

==================================================
56. PRINT CHECKLIST

Create a simple checklist:

✓ Roster verified
✓ Seating verified
✓ Template verified
✓ Documents generated
✓ QR codes verified
✓ Signature lists generated
✓ Seating maps generated
✓ Package downloaded

This is an operational safeguard.

==================================================
57. REGISTRAR DASHBOARD

Improve the main dashboard.

Show:

Upcoming Exams
Draft Exams
Ready for Administration
Completed
Archived

For each:

course
exam
date
students
rooms
status

==================================================
58. EXAM SEARCH

Allow administrators to search:

course code
exam name
date
instructor

Use server-side filtering.

==================================================
59. FILTERING

Allow filters:

status
date
course
term

Do not build a complicated reporting engine.

==================================================
60. PAGINATION

Large lists must use pagination.

Do not load thousands of records into the browser unnecessarily.

==================================================
61. DATABASE QUERY REVIEW

Check for:

N+1 queries
unnecessary joins
full-table scans

Especially:

exam list
roster
seating
generated documents

Fix obvious issues only.

Do not prematurely optimize.

==================================================
62. BACKUP

Document the required backup strategy.

At minimum:

PostgreSQL database
protected document storage

must be backed up according to institutional policy.

Do not implement a custom backup system if infrastructure already provides one.

==================================================
63. RESTORE TEST

Document a restore procedure.

A backup that has never been restored is not a verified backup.

If possible:

test restoring a development/staging database from backup.

Do not perform destructive production testing.

==================================================
64. DISASTER RECOVERY

Document:

database loss
file storage loss
application server loss

and expected recovery process.

Do not invent unrealistic RTO/RPO values.

Mark them as institutional decisions if not defined.

==================================================
65. HEALTH CHECK

Implement or review:

GET /health

It should verify basic application availability.

Do not expose sensitive configuration.

==================================================
66. READINESS CHECK

If the deployment architecture supports it:

provide a readiness check that verifies:

database connectivity
required storage connectivity

Do not expose credentials.

==================================================
67. MONITORING

The application should expose useful operational metrics/logs.

Monitor:

application availability
database connectivity
generation failures
package generation failures
request errors
storage failures

Reuse existing institutional monitoring where possible.

==================================================
68. ALERTING

Do not create excessive alerts.

Useful alerts:

application unavailable
database unavailable
high generation failure rate
storage failure
disk space exhaustion

==================================================
69. DISK SPACE

If local temporary files are used for PDF/ZIP generation:

ensure temporary files are cleaned up.

Prevent disk exhaustion.

==================================================
70. TEMPORARY FILE CLEANUP

Every temporary generation operation must have cleanup logic.

Cleanup must happen:

on success
on failure
on cancellation where possible

==================================================
71. CONCURRENCY

Prevent two administrators from simultaneously performing conflicting
operations.

Examples:

two seating generations
two package generations
two template activations

Use simple locking or database constraints where appropriate.

Do not build distributed locking unless actually required.

==================================================
72. DUPLICATE GENERATION

If two users click:

Generate Package

at the same time:

do not create inconsistent results.

One operation should succeed or the system should safely recognize that the
package already exists.

==================================================
73. IMPORT CONCURRENCY

Do not allow one roster import to silently overwrite another user's changes.

Use explicit versioning or conflict detection where appropriate.

==================================================
74. TEST DATA

Create a repeatable seed/test dataset.

Example:

Exam:
TEST101

Students:
20

Rooms:
2

Seats:
20

This should be usable for development and automated tests.

Do not put real student information in test fixtures.

==================================================
75. SECURITY TEST DATA

Include tests for:

unauthorized user
authorized user
different exam
different room
different document

==================================================
76. AUTOMATED TEST COVERAGE

Ensure tests exist for critical business rules.

Prioritize:

authorization
seating integrity
document generation
QR token security
package generation
CSV validation
stale detection

Do not chase arbitrary percentage coverage.

==================================================
77. REGRESSION TESTS

Previous phase tests must continue passing.

Do not break:

Phase 7 PDF generation
Phase 8 QR generation
signature lists
seating maps

==================================================
78. API DOCUMENTATION

Ensure API documentation is clear.

For every important endpoint:

method
path
authentication
authorization
request
response
errors

Do not document internal implementation details.

==================================================
79. USER DOCUMENTATION

Create/update:

Administrator Guide

Include:

Create an exam
Import roster
Configure rooms
Assign seating
Import Crowdmark template
Generate personalized exams
Generate administration package
Download package
Regenerate after changes
Archive exam

